e human drivers are gone  and all of our main characters live  their lives entirely in their own Navi  bodies even the film's antagonists  aren't avatars they're Navi bodies with  human memories transplanted into their  brains so it really isn't an Avatar  movie it's more of a navi movie or  Pocahontas in space 2. all that aside I  really loved this movie and if you  haven't seen it yet or you saw it in 2D  I have to recommend you see it in 3D it  truly was meant to be seen this way if  you think 3D movies are bad or  unnecessary I thought the same thing but  decided Avatar was worth it and boy was  I right 3D technology has advanced so  much over the past 13 years since the  first Avatar film was released and it  really shows in the way of water just to  name a few reasons why this film was so  great the underwater scenes were  absolutely breathtaking no pun intended  Jay CE Cameron decided to not use dry  wet filming like you might see in movies  such as Aquaman since it gives an  unrealistic looking result instead they  used revolutionary underwater motion  capture technology which gave us some of  the best looking water scenes in movie  history it also LED Kate Winslet to beat  the record for the longest amount of  time for holding one's breath in a  filmmaking performance previously held  by Tom Cruise and Mission Impossible  Rogue Nation she smashed the record  holding her breath for over seven  minutes long I also loved the prologue  in the film which helped to catch us up  to what the Solly family had been up to  since we saw them last and gave us a  look into the Solly family's life with  babies being born and adopted and the  parents going out for a date night I  also appreciated the ending to the movie  where Jake opens his eyes in the exact  same way as the end of the first film to  the sound of beating drums I really  appreciated Sigourney Weaver's inclusion  in the film as well especially in the  character of Kiri and her relationship  with Dr Augustine kiri's connection to  Nature and the deity AWA was especially  fascinating to see and I hope it gets  explored more in the future Avatar films  Avatar the seed Bearer Avatar the token  Rider and Avatar the Quest for AWA  lastly I loved how the phrase a sun for  a sun was used in the film initially  natiri used it as a threat to kill  kwara's son's spider after her son  nateam was killed in the end though Jake  used the phrase to explain how their son  natam joined AWA in the afterlife and  how spider joined the soli family the  only other slight issues I had with the  film were how they seemingly just jumped  into speaking English when in the first  movie they spoke navi for the film's  entirety also the story felt very  similar to the original as the family  had to learn a new people's way of life  just like Jake learning the omit Takaya  people's way of life also in some cases  the battle scenes looked like they were  from a video game rather than a movie  Next the film was probably a bit too  long but I love long movies in a quote  from Empire magazine James Cameron said  I don't want anybody whining about  length when they sit in binge watch  television for eight hours I've watched  my kids sit and do five one hour  episodes in a row here's the big social  Paradigm [ __ ] that has to happen it's  okay to get up and go pee lastly the  solely family seems to be in almost the  exact same position as they were at the  beginning of the film since Colonel  korich doesn't ever die first he was  reincarnated in a navi body and then he  was saved from drowning by his not son's  son son you'd expect for the family to  be in a better position by the end of  the movie but they're in just about the  same exact spot as before regardless of  all of this and whether the way of water  is an Avatar movie I absolutely loved it  and highly recommend to anyone who  hasn't seen it  foreign  [Music]
 

